* 23 Nov 2026:

data/demand.csv now has one hourly demand column per day, headed by the
date (03-Aug-26, 09-Jan-26, 14-Jul-21, 19-Jan-21). run.py and the
notebook read it as a table and take 19-Jan-21, the original curve;
baseline min production cost and min emissions re-solve to the existing
runs/ values (0 and 100MW, to ~1e-15), so no rerun is needed.
The summer days were meant as a sensitivity study, but the model has no
feasible schedule for either: with lignite held flat all day, hydro, gas
and solar must follow the whole daily range of demand, and they span at most
1702-1802 MW (0-100MW new solar) against summer daily ranges of 1849 and
1895. Prices and CO2 do not enter, so the 2026 data gives the same
verdicts. New src/feasibility.py shows this (derived/demand_feasibility.csv,
paper/tables/demand_feasibility{,_captions}.tex,
paper/figures/demand_feasibility.pdf), checking every verdict with the LP.

* 12 Nov 2026:

New scenario update_2026: 2026 prices and coefficients changed
together, read from data/2026/ (DATA_DIRS in run.py) rather than patched
onto the baseline. data/2026/make_2026.py generates the files: hydro
cost 1.6; lignite 5.0 c/kWh, 1.6 kg CO2/kWh, minimum output 40%; gas
13.7; solar CO2 x1.6 (table and hourly profile); env and sus cost +10%.
Fuel prices are 2020 prices plus inflation only (+14.3%, as for hydro),
excluding CO2 charges: the source's 14.7 for lignite includes one, and
with it lignite is the dearest and dirtiest plant, so the min-cost and
min-emissions schedules coincide (cost and emissions spreads 0.01%).

* 10 Nov 2026:

Notebook no longer prints the full combined Pareto front as a LaTeX
table: with the epsilon-constraint sweep it is ~540 points per solar
size (was 44 before epsilon). It now writes
paper/tables/pareto_front_counts.tex (points contributed by each
method), pareto_front_representative_{solar}MW.tex (11 points evenly
spaced along production cost, plus the min-emissions point) and the full
fronts to derived/pareto_front_combined_{solar}MW.csv.

* 8 Nov 2026:

Alternative scenarios for reviewers (SCENARIOS in run.py, optional 4th
argument to run.py, RUN_SCENARIOS in run_exps.sh, analysis in
scenarios.py): New Solar distance 5/10/178.83 km; solar production cost
5.7 and 4.8 c/kWh (both solar plants); lignite CO2 1.6 kg/kWh.
Pareto archive now writes generations.npy into its run directory rather
than the working directory, so runs can go in parallel.
Fixed lp_solve's ABNORMAL fallback: when the fallback re-solve also
failed, it returned an all-zero schedule (no demand met, every objective
0), which dominated whole fronts. It now re-solves min production cost
from scratch, and raises if even that fails. Hit twice in
solar_cost_4.8 (grid_search_norm, pareto_archive seed 2, both 10MW);
those two runs were regenerated. Baseline runs never hit it.
scenarios.py refuses any result that does not meet demand.
lp_solve now retries a numerical failure (ABNORMAL) with GLOP without
presolve, then HiGHS, before the old min-production-cost fallback
(SOLVE_ATTEMPTS). Existing runs/ results were produced without this.

Fixed sensitivity.py stability intervals, which were wrong. It assumed
dispatch is a merit order, so a flip can only happen where two plants'
effective costs cross, and re-solved only at those crossings. The
lignite flat-output constraint couples the hours, so flips happen where
no costs cross (none of the true breakevens is at a crossing). Now
bisects on "is the baseline schedule still optimal" (cost vs re-solved
optimum; valid since the stable set is an interval, and immune to the
many near-tied optima). At 100MW the breakevens move from e.g. solar
-21%/+89% to -8.6%/+4.0%; every flip moves 100-330 MWh/day of ~138
GWh and changes CO2 by at most 0.35%. Breakevens now differ between
solar sizes. Table gains "moves (MWh/day)"; tornado axis adapts.

